  { days: '109', title: 'days: JEE Main 2027 Session 1 begins (22 January 2027)', body: 'NIFT is on 10 January and UCEED on 17 January, so design and JEE Main share the same month.', note: 'Registration for JEE Main is expected in October and November.' },
];

const planSteps = [
  { title: 'Name your situation', body: 'Use the A, B or C cards. A says what you hold. It decides which doors read it.' },
  { title: 'Pick two doors and one backup', body: 'One that fits how you work, one that fits your family budget, and one that costs little to keep alive, such as CUET for a BSc or IAT.' },
  { title: 'Run one small test on each', body: 'Design: finish one drawing or observation set each week. Research: solve one real problem set and one short Python project. Uniform or sea: book the medical. Give each test as long as it genuinely takes.' },
  { title: 'Write the decision down', body: 'One page: what you chose, what it costs, what you gave up and the date you will review it. Show it to your family.' },
];

const gatesItems = [
  { num: 'Gate 1', name: 'Proof of Skill', body: 'One finished piece for your door: a drawing set, a data analysis, a problem set solved end to end, or a medical certificate.' },
  { num: 'Gate 2', name: 'Proof of Communication', body: 'Explain that work in 30 seconds to two minutes to someone outside your field, in the language you use at home.' },
  { num: 'Gate 3', name: 'Proof of Value', body: 'Get three people who work in that field to look at it and tell you what they would change.' },
];

const parentRows = [
  { say: 'My JEE rank is bad, so I am giving up on a good college.', better: 'My rank only matters at four kinds of door. Here are the 12 doors that never read it.' },
  { say: 'I do not want engineering.', better: 'I checked 20 doors. Here are two, the dates, the budget and the backup.' },
  { say: 'Trust me.', better: 'Here is a one-page decision note and the date we review it.' },
];

const mistakes = [
  { title: 'Assuming JEE Main opens IISER or IISc BS', body: 'IISER takes IAT only. IISc BS takes JEE Advanced or IAT. A strong JEE Main percentile alone opens neither.' },
  { title: 'Using last year\'s score without reading the notice', body: 'Each door names its own year. The Navy asked for JEE Main 2025 in one notice and 2026 in the next. NATA states its rule. For JEE Main we found none.' },
  { title: 'Skipping NATA while aiming at B.Arch', body: 'Paper 2A and NATA are separate routes. If one attempt goes wrong, the other keeps architecture alive.' },
  { title: 'Paying a flying school before the medical', body: 'A failed Class 1 medical ends that route after the money is gone. Do the medical first.' },
  { title: 'Treating NDA as the only plan', body: 'About 20 shortlisted candidates compete for each NDA vacancy. Keep a second door alive.' },
  { title: 'Counting a service B.Tech as leaving engineering', body: 'The Navy and Army cadet entries read JEE Main and give a B.Tech. Pick them for the uniform, not as an escape.' },
];

const faqItems = [
  {
    q: 'What are the career options after 12th PCM other than engineering?',
    a: 'Architecture and planning (JEE Main Paper 2A or 2B, or NATA), research (IISER through IAT, NISER through NEST, IISc BS through JEE Advanced or IAT), design (UCEED, NID, NIFT), law (CLAT), IPM, a CUET BSc, the IIT Madras BS, NDA, pilot and merchant navy. Only a few of them read your JEE result.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I use my JEE Main score for a non-engineering course?',
    a: 'Yes, for a short list: B.Arch (Paper 2A), B.Planning (Paper 2B), a few integrated seats at IIITs and GFTIs, and the Navy and Army cadet entries that still give a B.Tech. Almost every other door runs its own test.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does IISER accept JEE Advanced?',
    a: 'No. IISER Pune\'s admissions page says admission is only through the IISER Aptitude Test. The JEE Advanced and KVPY channels ended before the 2024 intake. IISc BS does take JEE Advanced or IAT.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does IISc BS accept JEE Main?',
    a: 'Not on the 2026-27 selection page. It lists JEE Advanced 2026 and IAT 2026 for Indian candidates. The BS (Research) programme has 125 seats, including 20 extra seats for women.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is my 2026 JEE score valid next year?',
    a: 'We found no official validity text for JEE Main, so plan as if each result serves one counselling cycle. NATA is the exception: its brochure says a valid 2025 score still works for 2026-27 if you have not taken a seat. Read each door\'s notice for the score year it names.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I try NDA and also sit JEE Main?',
    a: 'Yes. They are different exams. NDA I 2027 is on 11 April 2027 (UPSC calendar, as reported) and JEE Main Session 1 is in January 2027, so a student can sit both. The NDA written paper has 300 Maths marks out of 900, which is where your JEE Maths helps.',
  },
  {
    q: 'I got under 50 percentile in JEE Main. What next?',
    a: 'Doors that never read it are open: IAT, NEST, UCEED, NID, NIFT, CLAT, CUET, NDA and the IIT Madras BS. The ones that read your result, such as B.Arch at NITs and SPAs, ask for ranks in the top few percent of Paper 2A.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need Chemistry for these doors?',
    a: 'Not for B.Arch: the Council of Architecture rule asks for Physics and Maths plus one more subject. IAT needs a science stream. NDA Navy, Air Force and Naval Academy ask for Physics and Maths.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the cheapest backup door to keep alive?',
    a: 'CUET for a BSc costs the least in money and time, and IAT shares preparation with IISc BS. Watch the NTA notice for CUET 2027 registration, which is expected in January.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why does this page print no salaries?',
    a: 'Because the pages we read gave no sample size, year or even currency for their pay claims. Ask each college for its median placement and how many students it covers, and ask a flying school for the last batch\'s real first-year offers.',
  },
];

const faqJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqItems.map((item) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: item.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: item.a },
  })),
};
---

<BlogPostLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  category="career-options"
  publishedAt={publishedDateISO}
  publishedLabel={publishedDateLabel}
  authorName={authorName}
  authorUrl={authorUrl}
  authorRole={authorRole}
  jsonLdExtras={[faqJsonLd]}
>
  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>Most career options after 12th PCM other than engineering never ask for your JEE result. Only a short list does: B.Arch, B.Planning and IISc BS. The rest (IISER, NDA, design, law, IPM, a CUET BSc) run their own tests.</strong>
    So the first question is not "which job". It is "does this door read my result, and for how long". Once you know that, you can build the proof that opens a high-value skill portfolio, stronger income options and earlier financial freedom, with or without a rank.
  </p>

  <figure class="article-visual article-cover-visual">
    <img src="/images/blog/career-options/career-options-after-12th-pcm-other-than-engineering/career-options-after-12th-pcm-other-than-engineering-cover.webp" alt="Indian PCM student weighing architecture, data, defence, and other routes beyond engineering" title="PCM career options after 12th other than engineering" width="1600" height="1000" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" />
    <figcaption>A JEE result is a filter for some doors, not a definition of what a PCM student can become.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li>We checked 20 non-engineering doors. Six read a JEE result, two more read it but still give you a B.Tech, and twelve ignore it.</li>
      <li>B.Arch is about 0.9% of JoSAA's 67,323 seats, and the four B.Arch seats we could check closed between Paper 2A ranks 207 and 1,983 last year.</li>
      <li>IISER takes IAT only, and IISc BS takes JEE Advanced or IAT. A JEE Main percentile alone opens neither.</li>
      <li><strong>Pick by what the door reads and the date it closes. Then build one finished piece of proof for it, because proof, not rank, is what widens your income options later.</strong></li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p>
    If you want the same doors sorted by subject, date and age instead, see
    <a href="/blog/career-options/career-options-after-12th-except-engineering/">career options after 12th except engineering</a>.
    For the engineering side of the same rank, see
    <a href="/blog/career-options/career-options-after-12th-pcm/">career options after 12th PCM</a>.
    The wider set of articles is in the
    <a href="/blog/career-options/">career options library</a>.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you are still unsure which door fits how you work, the
    <a href="/career-skills-compass/">Career &amp; Skills Compass</a> gives a quick read on your strengths.
  </p>

  <nav class="jump-nav" aria-label="Article sections">
    <p class="jump-nav__label">Jump to section</p>
    <ul class="jump-nav__list">
      {jumpLinks.map((item) => (
        <li><a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.label}</a></li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="ledger">Career options after 12th PCM other than engineering: what each door does with your JEE result</h2>

  <figure class="article-visual">
    <img src="/images/blog/career-options/career-options-after-12th-pcm-other-than-engineering/career-options-after-12th-pcm-other-than-engineering-ledger.webp" alt="JEE result ledger branching into architecture, NDA, data science, and other entrance routes" title="What each PCM career door does with your JEE result" width="1600" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
    <figcaption>Start by asking what each door reads: JEE Main, JEE Advanced, another exam, or only your subject and date.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    Tap a filter. The table cuts down to the doors that match what you hold.
  </p>

  <p>
    JoSAA is the joint counselling that fills IIT, NIT, IIIT and government institute seats. The order puts the doors that read a JEE result first, then the doors that ignore it, with the service cadet entries in the middle. Inside each group, doors where visible proof raises your pay come before doors where rank and seniority set it.
  </p>

  <div class="filter-bar" role="group" aria-label="Filter the doors">
    {filters.map((f, i) => (
      <button type="button" class="filter-chip" data-filter={f.key} aria-pressed={i === 0 ? 'true' : 'false'}>{f.label}</button>
    ))}
  </div>
  <p class="filter-count" aria-live="polite" id="filter-count">Showing all 20 doors.</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table doors-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Door</th>
          <th>Reads your JEE result?</th>
          <th>What it reads</th>
          <th>Next date</th>
          <th>Seats and crowd</th>
          <th>Gate to check</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {doors.map((d) => (
          <tr data-tags={d.tags}>
            <td data-label="Door"><strong>{d.name}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Reads your JEE result?"><span class={`verdict verdict--${d.vclass}`}>{d.verdict}</span></td>
            <td data-label="What it reads">{d.reads}</td>
            <td data-label="Next date">{d.next}</td>
            <td data-label="Seats and crowd">{d.seats}</td>
            <td data-label="Gate to check">{d.gate}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Sources: IISER Pune admissions page, the IISc BS selection page, the UCEED 2027 page, the NATA 2026 brochure, and NTA, JoSAA and news reports for the rest. Where a count could not be confirmed we say so. Dates for 2027 that are not published are marked as expected.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      Holding a JEE result does not shrink your choices. It tells you which eight doors can use it (six give a non-engineering degree, two still give a B.Tech). The other twelve only need a date and a plan.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="share">How small the JEE-reading corner of JoSAA really is</h2>

  <p>
    JoSAA is the big counselling that fills IIT, NIT, IIIT and government institute seats from JEE ranks. Almost all of it is engineering.
  </p>

  <div class="feature-grid feature-grid--stats">
    {shareRows.map((r) => (
      <div class="feature-card">
        <div class="feature-card__label">{r.part}</div>
        <h3 class="stat-num">{r.num}</h3>
        <p>{r.note}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <div class="callout callout--amber">
    <p class="callout__eyebrow">Why this matters for your choice</p>
    <ul class="callout__list">
      <li>If you want to leave engineering and also use your rank, the pool is under 1% of the seats JoSAA sells. That is a small door, not a general exit.</li>
      <li>In 2026 IISc joined JoSAA for four B.Tech programmes using JEE Advanced. Its BS (Research) is a separate route, so do not mix them up.</li>
      <li>BS and integrated MSc seats exist at a few IITs, but we could not confirm seat counts from the pages we could read. Search the programme name in the official seat matrix.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="callout__footer">
      The 600 B.Arch figure is a coaching-site estimate that includes IIT seats. Treat the 0.9% as a rough size, not an exact one.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="ranks">B.Arch ranks turned into seat odds</h2>

  <figure class="article-visual">
    <img src="/images/blog/career-options/career-options-after-12th-pcm-other-than-engineering/career-options-after-12th-pcm-other-than-engineering-seats.webp" alt="Editorial comparison of rank, eligibility rules, and seat availability for architecture choices" title="Read rank, rule, and seat odds separately" width="1600" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
    <figcaption>Rank, eligibility, and seat availability are related but different filters; keep them separate when reading an opportunity.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    B.Arch is the one non-engineering JoSAA door with enough published data to do the maths. Here is where the 2025 Round 6 closing ranks fall inside the 2026 test-taker pool.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Institute</th>
          <th>Closing rank, 2025 Round 6 (open, gender-neutral)</th>
          <th>Working</th>
          <th>Share of 45,452 Paper 2A test-takers</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {rankRows.map((r) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Institute"><strong>{r.inst}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Closing rank, 2025 Round 6 (open, gender-neutral)">{r.close}</td>
            <td data-label="Working">{r.work}</td>
            <td data-label="Share of 45,452 Paper 2A test-takers">{r.share}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Read it like this. A seat at SPA Delhi or NIT Calicut needed a rank in roughly the top half of one percent. MNIT Jaipur reached about the top 4%.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p class="callout__eyebrow">How to read these numbers safely</p>
    <ul class="callout__list">
      <li>The 45,452 is Session 1 only, while ranks pool both sessions. So the true share is a little higher than shown.</li>
      <li>Ranks are from 2025 and the test-taker pool is from 2026. Use them to see the scale, not to predict your seat.</li>
      <li>Sources disagree on IIT Kharagpur and IIT Roorkee closing ranks (one table shows about 17,000 to 19,000 on JEE Advanced, another shows 1,500 to 2,800 for Roorkee). Read the JoSAA closing-rank page itself for those two.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="callout__footer">
      The same rank tables are published after every JoSAA round. Look at the last round, not the first.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="life">How long your JEE result stays useful, door by door</h2>

  <p>
    A JEE result is not a permanent pass. Each door reads a specific year, and some of them have already closed for this cycle.
  </p>

  <div class="protocol-stack">
    {lifeSteps.map((item) => (
      <div class="checkpoint">
        <div class="checkpoint__num checkpoint__num--word">{item.num}</div>
        <div class="checkpoint__body">
          <div class="checkpoint__title">{item.title}</div>
          <p>{item.body}</p>
          <div class="checkpoint__note">{item.note}</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2 id="service">Two doors that read JEE but still give you a B.Tech</h2>

  <p>
    The Navy and Army cadet entries use a JEE Main result, so they show up in every "after JEE" list. But the degree is engineering.
  </p>

  <div class="feature-grid">
    {serviceCards.map((c) => (
      <div class="feature-card">
        <div class="feature-card__label">{c.label}</div>
        <h3>{c.title}</h3>
        <p>{c.body}</p>
        <p class="feature-card__fit"><span>{c.fit.split(':')[0]}:</span>{c.fit.slice(c.fit.indexOf(':') + 1)}</p>
        <p class="feature-card__watch"><span>{c.watch.split(':')[0]}:</span>{c.watch.slice(c.watch.indexOf(':') + 1)}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      The three service cadet courses together had 194 vacancies in one cycle (44 Navy in July 2026, 60 Navy for January 2027, 90 Army). That is a tiny number next to the roughly 15.4 lakh unique candidates who sat JEE Main 2026 (a headline figure from a news report).
    </p>
    <p>
      Treat these as one strong attempt, not a plan. Keep a second door alive that does not read JEE, and keep the medical and SSB rules in view from day one.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="nda">What your JEE months cover in the NDA written paper</h2>

  <p>
    NDA ignores JEE, but your JEE Maths is not wasted. Here is how much of the NDA scoring it can reach.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>NDA part</th>
          <th>Marks</th>
          <th>Share of written marks</th>
          <th>Share of the 1,800 final</th>
          <th>Does JEE prep help?</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {ndaRows.map((r) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="NDA part"><strong>{r.part}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Marks">{r.marks}</td>
            <td data-label="Share of written marks">{r.written}</td>
            <td data-label="Share of the 1,800 final">{r.final}</td>
            <td data-label="Does JEE prep help?">{r.help}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    The working is simple: 300 / 900 = 33.3% of the written paper, and 300 / 1,800 = 16.7% of the final merit. Two thirds of the written marks, and half of the final score, sit outside what JEE trains.
  </p>

  <p>
    About 7,900 candidates cleared the NDA I 2026 written round for the SSB, against 394 vacancies reported for NDA II 2026. That is about 20 per vacancy (7,903 / 394). The two numbers come from different cycles, so use the ratio as a guide only.
  </p>

  <p>
    Build a parallel skill while you prepare, such as Python or a small design portfolio, so a missed SSB does not leave the year empty.
  </p>

  <h2 id="iitm">The IIT degree that does not read JEE: IIT Madras BS</h2>

  <p>
    If the IIT name matters to your family, there is one route that skips JEE completely. It is slower to start and it filters by coursework, not by one exam.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">How it works</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Four programmes: Data Science and Applications, Electronic Systems, Management and Data Science, Aeronautics and Space Technology.</li>
        <li>Data and Management programmes take Class 12 from any stream. Electronic Systems and Aeronautics ask for Physics and Maths.</li>
        <li>You do a four-week foundation course, then an in-person qualifier exam.</li>
        <li>You can exit with a foundation certificate, a diploma, a BSc or continue to the full BS.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">What to check first</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Total fee is about Rs 3.86 to 6.12 lakh over the programme (secondary source). Confirm it on the IIT Madras portal.</li>
        <li>The September-term window closed on 27 September 2026. Look for the next term's dates.</li>
        <li>An online BS is a different experience from a campus B.Tech. Placement outcomes are not the same, and we found no official placement data for it.</li>
        <li>For Electronic Systems and Aeronautics, the Physics and Maths rule is where your PCM helps.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="rule-check">What pages say vs what the notice says</h2>

  <p>
    Pages that rank for this topic repeat the same mix-ups about JEE. Here they are next to the rule.
  </p>

  <div class="rule-list">
    {ruleRows.map((r) => (
      <div class="rule-row">
        <div class="rule-row__claim"><span>You will read</span>{r.claim}</div>
        <div class="rule-row__rule"><span>The notice says</span>{r.rule}</div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2 id="situations">Which student are you right now</h2>

  <p>
    The same ledger gives three different plans, depending on what you hold today.
  </p>

  <div class="feature-grid">
    {situationCards.map((c) => (
      <div class="feature-card">
        <div class="feature-card__label">{c.label}</div>
        <h3>{c.title}</h3>
        <p>{c.body}</p>
        <p class="feature-card__fit"><span>{c.fit.split(':')[0]}:</span>{c.fit.slice(c.fit.indexOf(':') + 1)}</p>
        <p class="feature-card__watch"><span>{c.watch.split(':')[0]}:</span>{c.watch.slice(c.watch.indexOf(':') + 1)}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <p>
    A realistic week for Situation B is school and boards first, then a few short evening blocks and one longer weekend block for a single door. For Situation A, add a fixed weekly proof task for each door on your list.
  </p>

  <h2 id="checkpoint">Use The 4-Checkpoint Protocol before you pick a door</h2>

  <figure class="article-visual">
    <img src="/images/blog/career-options/career-options-after-12th-pcm-other-than-engineering/career-options-after-12th-pcm-other-than-engineering-checkpoint.webp" alt="Four-checkpoint worksheet for choosing a PCM route labelled fit, open, headroom, and test" title="Four-checkpoint PCM career protocol" width="1600" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
    <figcaption>Use fit, open doors, headroom, and a small test before turning an exam result into a one-year commitment.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    Before you commit, check your Biology, Context, Market and Survival. This is The 4-Checkpoint Protocol.
  </p>

  <div class="protocol-stack">
    {checkpointItems.map((item) => (
      <div class="checkpoint">
        <div class="checkpoint__num">{item.num}</div>
        <div class="checkpoint__body">
          <div class="checkpoint__title">{item.title}</div>
          <p>{item.body}</p>
          <div class="checkpoint__note">{item.note}</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2 id="ceilings">Which doors have headroom, and what caps the rest</h2>

  <p>
    Several doors here have two hard filters: getting in, then the first strong placement, licence or rank. Count both.
  </p>

  <div class="feature-grid">
    {ceilingCards.map((c) => (
      <div class="feature-card">
        <div class="feature-card__label">{c.label}</div>
        <h3>{c.title}</h3>
        <p>{c.body}</p>
        <p class="feature-card__fit"><span>{c.fit.split(':')[0]}:</span>{c.fit.slice(c.fit.indexOf(':') + 1)}</p>
        <p class="feature-card__watch"><span>{c.watch.split(':')[0]}:</span>{c.watch.slice(c.watch.indexOf(':') + 1)}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      Of these doors, design and data give you the most control over your own income, because you can show work before anyone gives you a title. A working designer or analyst can freelance, build a small studio or consult. Those are ownership paths that scale with your proof.
    </p>
    <p>
      A stable salary alone rarely reaches early financial freedom. Add one skill you can sell on the side.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="money">Cost, the 10% rule and fee maths</h2>

  <p>
    A college should not eat the whole education budget. As a strict planning rule, treat about 10% of the total education budget as the starting cap for college fees, and about 20% as a caution line. These are planning rules, not laws.
  </p>

